"""
skills_library.py  -  a bilingual (English + French) keyword library for keyword_extractor.py
                      Une bibliothèque de mots-clés bilingue (anglais + français).

WHAT IT IS / CE QUE C'EST
    All the word lists used by keyword_extractor.py, kept in their own file so the
    library can grow without touching the program.
    Toutes les listes de mots utilisées par keyword_extractor.py, dans un fichier à part
    pour que la bibliothèque puisse grandir sans toucher au programme.

WHAT IS INSIDE / CONTENU
    SIZE    43 domains, 2,446 skills, 20,043 keywords (English + French)
    PART 1  LIBRARY          work domain -> skills.
                             One skill = English name -> (French name, [ways to write it, EN + FR])
    PART 2  SKILLS           the same skills as a flat dict: English name -> [aliases]
                             (exactly the format the old keyword_extractor.py used)
            NAMES_FR         English name -> French name
            DOMAIN_OF        English name -> domain id
    PART 3  PREFERRED_WORDS  words that make a skill "nice to have" on its line
            JOB_SECTIONS     job-post headings (EN + FR) -> section tag
            CV_HEADINGS      CV headings (EN + FR) -> section tag
            FLAG_PATTERNS    eligibility items to VERIFY (never scored)
            RELATED          skills that LOOK related but are NOT counted as a match
    PART 4  clean()          the text cleaning shared by the library and the extractor
            check / export   python3 skills_library.py           -> health check
                             python3 skills_library.py export    -> skills_library.json + .csv

THE DOMAINS (43, loosely following the Canadian NOC / CNP broad categories)
    Engineering (industrial, quality, supply chain, manufacturing, mechanical, electrical,
    civil, chemical, aerospace, energy), safety, software, data and AI, IT, cybersecurity,
    project management, business, finance, HR, marketing, sales, legal, health, life
    sciences, education, social services, arts and media, hospitality, retail, trades,
    agriculture, mining, transportation, government, security, real estate, office
    support, soft skills, languages, certifications, biomedical engineering,
    sports and fitness, personal and domestic services.

RULES FOR WRITING ALIASES / RÈGLES POUR ÉCRIRE LES ALIAS
    1. Lowercase. Accents are optional: "génie" and "genie" are the same after cleaning.
    2. A space or a hyphen in an alias matches a space OR a hyphen
       ("problem solving" also finds "problem-solving").
    3. A final "s" is optional, so write the singular. Irregular plurals
       ("analyses", "files d'attente") must be written out.
    4. Whole words only: "sap" is not found inside "sapphire".
    5. AVOID short or common words that mean something else, especially in French:
         "it", "ai" (j'ai), "mes" (my), "tableau" (table), "notion", "chef", "cet",
         "qc" (Québec), "pm" (2 pm), "ml" (millilitre), "vue" (view), "tps" (GST),
         "pv" (procès-verbal), "fluent" (fluent in French), "transformer", "pipeline",
         "concrete", "leader", "r", "c", "go".
       Write a longer phrase instead: "tableau software", "golang", "machine learning".
    6. Known trade-offs kept on purpose (a word that is usually the skill):
         "excel", "react", "rust", "swift", "flask", "api", "arena", "unity3d".
       If one gives false hits in your data, replace it with a longer phrase.

ONE SKILL = ONE PLACE
    Each English name exists only once in the whole library. The same alias should not
    belong to two skills: run the health check after every edit.
"""

LIBRARY = {}   # filled domain by domain below


